    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The colony existed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> depended on Native nations to capture, skin and deliver beaver pelts, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> conquered the Mahicans in 1628 they held a near-monopoly of that trade.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-minuit">Peter Minuit</a> acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> from unnamed Native people on 24 May 1626 for goods worth 60 guilders, a figure a historian turned into the famous $24 in 1846 by a faulty conversion.</li><li>Its 1664 Articles of Capitulation kept the liberty of conscience New Netherlanders already enjoyed, and the Flushing Remonstrance of 1657 is counted a precursor of the religious-freedom clause in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights">United States Bill of Rights</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>'s wal, the rampart that gave Wall Street its name, was built out of fear of an English invasion; when four English frigates finally came in 1664 the town surrendered without resistance.</p></aside>
    </section>

