    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-hudson.jpg" alt="Henry Hudson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson">Henry Hudson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HenryHudson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1609 the English captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-hudson">Henry Hudson</a>, hired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> to find a passage to Asia, sailed the flyboat Halve Maen into Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Bay and up the river now named for him until the water grew too shallow at the site of Troy. He reported fertile land and people willing to barter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. Between 1611 and 1614 Adriaen Block, Hendrick Christiaensen and Cornelius Jacobsen May charted the coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> to Massachusetts, and Block's map was the first to carry the name New Netherland. Juan Rodriguez, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> of Portuguese and African descent, wintered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> in 1613–1614 trading for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, the first recorded non-Native inhabitant of the future New York City.</p><p>On 17 March 1614 the States General offered an exclusive patent for trade between the 40th and 45th parallels, won by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-netherland-company">New Netherland Company</a>, whose traders built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-nassau-berbice">Fort Nassau</a> on Castle Island near Albany; flooding forced its abandonment when the patent expired in 1618. On 3 June 1621 the Republic chartered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> with exclusive rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. In 1624 New Netherland became a province, and in May the company landed 30 families at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-orange">Fort Orange</a> and Noten Eylant from the Nieu Nederlandt under May, its first director; 45 more colonists and 103 animals followed in 1625. Many settlers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloons">Walloons</a>, French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> or Africans, most of the last enslaved.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam.jpg" alt="New Amsterdam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMH-6831-KB_View_of_New_Amsterdam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacob van Meurs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like the French to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> came for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. Their first partners were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algonquin-people">Algonquins</a>, and they depended on Native nations to capture, skin and deliver pelts, above all beaver; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> of the Iroquois Confederacy conquered the Mahicans in 1628, they held a near-monopoly of the trade through the Adirondacks and the Mohawk Valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, outside Fort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, became a hub for trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, loading pelts, lumber and tobacco. The Charter of Freedoms and Exemptions of 1629 granted vast patroonships with manorial courts and the duty to settle 50 tenants in four years; of the five, only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rensselaerswyck">Rensselaerswyck</a>, at the head of navigation on the North River, truly succeeded.</p><p>The colony's people were never only Dutch. Not counting Native Americans, the population was 4,301 in 1650 and 8,000 to 9,000 at the transfer of 1674; Dutch was the official language beside French, Scandinavian and German tongues and Algonquian languages. The company introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1625 with eleven enslaved Africans who worked as farmers, fur traders and builders. They were admitted to the Reformed Church, could testify in court, sign documents and sue whites, some earned wages equal to white workers', and families were usually kept together; some later gained "half-free" status, and when the colony fell the company freed its slaves, forming an early nucleus of free Black people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fort-christina"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-christina.jpg" alt="Fort Christina" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fort-christina">Fort Christina</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Model_of_Fort_Cristina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stilfehler, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>New Netherland was a chain of garrisoned trading stations rather than an armed state. Company policy required land to be bought from the Indians, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took their gifts of sewant and goods as exclusive title while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> bands meant to share, not leave, and the misunderstanding bred violence. Director <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-kieft">Willem Kieft</a> (1638–1647) demanded tribute from the Tappan and Wecquaesgeek, who ignored him, then, against the advice of his own Council of Twelve Men, ordered attacks at Pavonia and Corlear's Hook on groups who had taken refuge with the Dutch from their Mohican enemies. The massacre left 130 dead; the surrounding tribes united and raided the province for two years, until a treaty brokered largely by the Hackensack sagamore Oratam ended Kieft's War in 1645.</p><p>On the Delaware the patroons' settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwaanendael">Zwaanendael</a> was destroyed by the local Indigenous population soon after its founding in 1631, and in 1638 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-minuit">Peter Minuit</a>, now in Swedish service, planted New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-christina">Fort Christina</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> could only protest. The Dutch built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-casimir">Fort Casimir</a> in 1651, lost it to the Swedes in 1654, and in 1655 Stuyvesant led the expedition that conquered New Sweden, renaming its chief settlement New Amstel. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the colony had no defence: appeals for troops were ignored, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nicolls">Richard Nicolls</a>'s four frigates arrived on 27 August 1664 New Amsterdam surrendered without a shot.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant.jpg" alt="Peter Stuyvesant" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petrus_(Peter_Pieter)_Stuyvesant_portrait_c1660.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown painter (attributed to Hendrick Couturier, in the past was considered a Rembrandt), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>New Netherland was a company colony. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> appointed its directors, Cornelius Jacobsen May in 1624, Willem Verhulst in 1625, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-minuit">Peter Minuit</a> from 1626, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-kieft">Willem Kieft</a> from 1638 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a> from 1647, the only one styled director-general. Director and council together held executive and legislative power and sat as the court of justice. The colonists, vocal and quick to challenge authority, submitted the Remonstrance of New Netherland, written by the Leiden-educated lawyer Adriaen van der Donck, condemning the company's mismanagement and demanding full rights as citizens of the Netherlands; after three years' wrangling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> government granted a measure of self-government and recalled Stuyvesant in April 1652, orders rescinded a month later when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-dutch-war">First Anglo-Dutch War</a> broke out. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> got its municipal charter in 1653.</p><p>The company made the Reformed Church the official institution, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a>'s rule that no one be persecuted for religion was written into the first instructions of 1624: colonists were to win Indians and non-believers "through attitude and by example" and leave everyone the freedom of his conscience. The rule prevailed twice against Stuyvesant's narrow outlook, in the grant of full residency to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> and Sephardi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in New Amsterdam in 1655 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flushing-queens">Flushing</a> Remonstrance of 1657, when some thirty residents petitioned against his ban on Quaker worship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas.jpg" alt="Saint Nicholas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaroslav_%C4%8Cerm%C3%A1k_(1831_-_1878)_-_Sv._Mikul%C3%A1%C5%A1_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jaroslav Čermák, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony's reach was commercial and lasting. Its trade tied the Iroquois and Algonquian fur country to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, and its claims shaped the map: by the Treaty of Hartford of 1650 Stuyvesant provisionally ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, setting the eastern border 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> miles west of the river's mouth and just west of Oyster Bay on Long Island, a line the company refused to recognise but which held in practice. New Netherland grew into what is now the largest metropolitan area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and writers have argued that its "secular broadmindedness and mercantile pragmatism" and its financial rather than religious motive set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on a different course from the other colonies; William Byrd remarked in 1682 that it had as many sects as Amsterdam.</p><p>Its legal legacy ran through the Articles of Capitulation of 1664, whose Article VIII kept the liberty of conscience and which were incorporated into later city, state and national constitutions, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flushing-queens">Flushing</a> Remonstrance, counted a precursor of the Bill of Rights' provision on religious freedom. The Roosevelts descend from Claes van Roosevelt, who came about 1650; Martin Van Buren grew up in Kinderhook speaking only Dutch. The flags of New York City, Albany and Jersey City carry the colours of the Prinsenvlag, the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus">Santa Claus</a>, and Dutch names such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a>, Catskill and Red Hook remain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-andros.jpg" alt="Edmund Andros" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Edmund Andros</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Edmund_Andros_by_Beale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mary Beale, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony was always outgrown. English settlers from Massachusetts Bay moved onto the Fresh River and Long Island from 1637, with or without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> leave, and the New England Confederation of 1643 took in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and New Haven on land the United Provinces claimed; the Dutch could neither people nor defend their claim. In March 1664 Charles II resolved to annex New Netherland and bring all his kingdoms under one government in church and state. The company's directors hoped the religious freedom they offered would keep the English settlers loyal, but when Nicolls's frigates came on 27 August 1664 the defenceless town yielded, Stuyvesant negotiating good terms from his "too powerful enemies". On the Delaware Colonel Sir Robert Carr broke them, seizing property and selling Dutch prisoners of war into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and Pieter Corneliszoon Plockhoy's Mennonite settlement near Lewes was destroyed.</p><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> of 1667 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a>; the Dutch did not press their claim, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> island of Run instead. In August 1673 Evertsen and Binckes retook the colony, Anthony Colve governed it and the city was renamed New Orange, but the Republic, exhausted by the disaster years of 1672–1674, gave it up by the Treaty of Westminster in February 1674; on 10 November 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-andros">Edmund Andros</a> took over from Colve. Dutch speech, law and 8,000 to 9,000 New Netherlanders passed into the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>New Netherland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>'s colony on the Hudson and Delaware from the patent of 1614, lived by the beaver trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, grew fast in the 1650s around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, and fell without a fight to English frigates in 1664; the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> of 1667 left it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with its liberty of conscience intact.</em></p>

